"Robots That Take Initiative: A Framework for Building and Evaluating Proactive Robots (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.28910)\nciteturn26academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Title: Robots That Take Initiative: A Framework for Building and Evaluating Proactive Robots ... We then show that offline evaluation overstates performance in this setting, and contribute a closed-loop evaluation with a human model that adapts to the robot.\nTitle: Robots That Take Initiative: A Framework for Building and Evaluating Proactive Robots\nAuthors: Maithili Patel, Sonia Chernova\nDate: Thu Sep 24 01:42:09 2026\n\nEffective robot assistance beyond narrow roles and repetitive tasks requires robots to be proactive - to decide what needs to be done rather than waiting to be told. While proactivity is increasingly explored, it lacks a unified formulation, and work in the domain is typically evaluated offline against static human models that cannot capture the effect of a robot's actions on the environment and the user's own behavior. We introduce a unified formalism for proactive robot assistance, organize it into three levels, and provide a framework to address the highest level of unprompted proactive assistance. We then show that offline evaluation overstates performance in this setting, and contribute a closed-loop evaluation with a human model that adapts to the robot. Finally, we present a method, GAP, that instantiates our framework, learning from passive observation to anticipate user goals and act. Under closed-loop evaluation, prior state-of-the-art methods collapse, in some cases adding more work than they save, while GAP remains robust and substantially outperforms them.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRobots That Take Initiative: A Framework for Building and Evaluating Proactive Robots | AI Research Paper Details (https://www.aimodels.fyi/papers/arxiv/robots-that-take-initiative-framework-building-evaluating)\nciteturn26search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; # Robots That Take Initiative: A Framework for Building and Evaluating Proactive Robots ... The paper’s counterfactual value formulation complements a broader proactive service framework that also treats initiative as a decision under uncertainty rather than as frequent action. ... The paper’s concern about intervention cost also parallels work on proactive agent training, although the present evaluation measures physical task consequences in a robot environment.\n\n# Robots That Take Initiative: A Framework for Building and Evaluating Proactive Robots\n\nWhat happens when robots stop waiting for instructions and start deciding what you need?\n\nPublished 9/25/2026 by Maithili Patel, Sonia Chernova\n\n## GAP turns unprompted assistance into a testable robotics problem\n\nGAP, short for Grounding, Anticipation, and Planning, is the paper’s concrete method for robots that act without a supplied goal. It learns from passive activity logs, uses an LLM to organize goals, infers current tasks with a particle filter, predicts future goals with a causal transformer, and selects object relocations through a learned heuristic planner.\n\nThe central formalism treats assistance as a change relative to a counterfactual trajectory in which the human acts alone. Robot effectiveness is measured through ΔV−ΔC: added goal value minus changes in human and robot cost. A robot can save effort, enable otherwise missed goals, or create corrective work when its placement conflicts with the user’s plan. The paper’s counterfactual value formulation complements a broader proactive service framework that also treats initiative as a decision under uncertainty rather than as frequent action.\n\nThe framework separates three levels according to missing information. L0 Reactive Assistance receives both the goal and human policy; L1 Collaborative Proactive Assistance knows the goal but must infer how the person acts; L2 Unprompted Proactive Assistance receives neither and must infer needs from state and interaction history. The paper focuses on L2 because it removes the burden of delegating tasks.\n\nImage: Diagram of environment, human hidden state, goals, joint actions, and three increasing proactivity levels The formal model connects joint human–robot actions with hidden user state and goal generation, then orders assistance by how much information the robot lacks.\n\n## Grounding, anticipation, and planning divide the hard parts\n\nFor L2, the authors decompose the problem into grounding, anticipation, and planning. Grounding curates a goal space and assigns observed actions to goals; anticipation learns human policies, infers the current goal, and predicts future goals; planning estimates action costs and coordinates robot placements against anticipated behavior.\n\nGAP’s grounding stage uses gpt-4o-mini to maintain an open-vocabulary goal list and label daily action intervals. Each observed goal becomes a directed task graph whose nodes are object movements, while a particle filter tracks open hypotheses, completed steps, timing priors, and robot-satisfied nodes. Ambiguous actions can remain unassigned rather than receiving forced labels.\n\nA causal transformer predicts the next goal and its start time from curated goal segments, while the planner proposes placements for current and future tasks. Preconditions, lead-time thresholds, action confidence, and per-object acceptance estimates filter candidates. Recency weighting and retraining every five days let the system adapt to changing household routines without treating every historical observation as equally relevant. The placement stage is related in spirit to grounded vision-language planning, although GAP learns household routines and acceptance from passive observation.\n\n## Closed-loop evaluation exposes failures hidden by replay\n\nThe evaluation runs in VirtualHome with HOMER+ routines from three simulated households over several weeks. An LLM-based user model, gpt-4o-mini, adapts when the robot moves an object: it may accept the placement, undo it, or redirect the object. This creates state distribution shift and user behavior changes that static replay cannot represent. The simulated adaptation setting complements empirical work on human-robot collaboration by making the robot’s intervention effects explicit over longer household routines.\n\nOffline evaluation replays a fixed human sequence and therefore assumes that future states and goals remain unchanged after robot intervention. The closed-loop setup instead counts skipped actions as saved effort, undo actions as added work, corrected actions as perturbations, and reports each component separately alongside F1. The design uses a two-hour predictive window for adaptation decisions.\n\nImage: GAP pipeline connected to a closed-loop human adaptation evaluation with feedback arrows GAP combines grounding, anticipation, and planning inside an evaluation loop where robot actions alter both the environment and subsequent user behavior.\n\nThe comparison includes SLaTe-PRO, STREAK, a plain LLM predictor, and an oracle that acts one tick before ground-truth relocations. Under the LLM user, STREAK reaches −159.9% net actions saved and Plain LLM reaches −174.5%, showing that continual prediction or general language-model priors can compound errors when their own actions alter later observations.\n\n## GAP remains stable where prior predictors collapse\n\nGAP outperforms every non-oracle baseline across the closed-loop metrics except perturbed actions under the LLM user, where STREAK records fewer perturbations. That isolated advantage is misleading: STREAK’s low perturbation count accompanies strongly negative net savings, whereas GAP balances saved and disturbed actions through acceptance estimates, timing control, and goal-level structure. GAP’s acceptance estimates resemble the personalization goal of adaptive assistance policies, but this paper evaluates adaptation through object relocations and closed-loop user responses.\n\nThe reported GAP results are 17.7% ± 1.1 net actions saved for the scripted user and 30.8% ± 1.8 for the LLM user. Future-goal assistance supplies 32.8% of GAP actions with the scripted user and 25.3% with the LLM user, while precision is lower for future than current assistance: 0.64 versus 0.80, and 0.53 versus 0.62.\n\nRelative to SLaTe-PRO and STREAK, GAP is designed around the intervention loop rather than a fixed dataset distribution. Its task graphs remain useful when the robot completes a step, and its planner learns which placements users accept or reject. The authors also report that discarding rejected robot actions reduces net savings by 14.3 percentage points and raises perturbations by 19.1 points on the scripted user.\n\n## What the formalism leaves outside the current evaluation\n\nThe strongest limitation is the human model’s scope: it can adapt only among goals already present in HOMER+, rather than inventing genuinely new goals over long-term interaction. The authors state that real user studies are needed to validate the LLM proxy, because the simulated adaptation model cannot establish how people change goals and routines in deployed homes.\n\nThe evaluation also assumes uniform action costs except for extra recovery cost after perturbations. That simplification omits personal effort, preferences, interaction overhead, fatigue, and context-dependent disturbance, even though the formalism explicitly identifies these as components of human cost. The paper therefore demonstrates robust relative assistance under its simulator rather than a complete real-world value model. The paper’s concern about intervention cost also parallels work on proactive agent training, although the present evaluation measures physical task consequences in a robot environment.\n\nGAP’s result is specific to object relocation in VirtualHome, HOMER+ routines, and the stated closed-loop user models. Its advantage comes from combining current-goal help with future-goal preparation, but future prediction remains less precise. The contribution is consequently a unified formulation, an adaptive evaluation protocol, and a concrete pipeline whose failures are measured as user work rather than hidden by offline replay.\n\n## Original Paper\n\nView on arxiv(opens in a new tab)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPRM-as-a-Judge (https://prm-as-a-judge.github.io/blog-introducing-prm-as-a-judge.html)\nciteturn26search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: last month; # Image: PRM-as-a-Judge Introducing PRM-as-a-Judge: Dense Auditing for Robots ... As robotic manipulation graduates from simple \"pick-and-place\" to complex, contact-rich marathons, relying solely on Success Rate is like trying to judge a symphony by its final note.\n\nPRM-as-a-Judge\n\nPRM-as-a-Judge\n\n# Image: PRM-as-a-Judge Introducing PRM-as-a-Judge: Dense Auditing for Robots\n\nA dense evaluation paradigm that turns trajectory videos into interpretable signals for progress depth, execution quality, and failure mechanisms in long-horizon robotics.\n\nPublished March 23, 2026 Updated March 30, 2026 Report2603.21669 RepositoryPRM-as-a-Judge BenchmarkRoboPulse By PRM-as-a-Judge Team\n\nRead paper Jump to explorer View code\n\nTL;DR\n\nBinary success rate hides where a policy actually progressed and how it failed. PRM-as-a-Judge converts rollout videos into dense OPD signals that expose progression depth, execution quality, and failure mechanisms. This makes model comparison and diagnosis more transparent, actionable, and reproducible.\n\n## 1. Why Success Rate is No Longer the Full Story\n\nAs robotic manipulation graduates from simple \"pick-and-place\" to complex, contact-rich marathons, relying solely on Success Rate is like trying to judge a symphony by its final note. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPRM-as-a-Judge: A Dense Evaluation Paradigm for Fine-Grained Robotic Auditing (https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.21669)\nciteturn26academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Title: PRM-as-a-Judge: A Dense Evaluation Paradigm for Fine-Grained Robotic Auditing ... To address this limitation, we propose PRM-as-a-Judge, a dense evaluation paradigm that leverages Process Reward Models (PRMs) to audit policy execution directly from trajectory videos by estimating task progress from observation sequences.\nTitle: PRM-as-a-Judge: A Dense Evaluation Paradigm for Fine-Grained Robotic Auditing\nAuthors: Yuheng Ji, Yuyang Liu, Huajie Tan, Xuchuan Huang, Fanding Huang, Yijie Xu, Cheng Chi, Yuting Zhao, Huaihai Lyu, Peterson Co, Mingyu Cao, Qiongyu Zhang, Zhe Li, Enshen Zhou, Pengwei Wang, Zhongyuan Wang, Shanghang Zhang, Xiaolong Zheng\nDate: Mon Mar 23 07:48:42 2026\n\nCurrent robotic evaluation is still largely dominated by binary success rates, which collapse rich execution processes into a single outcome and obscure critical qualities such as progress, efficiency, and stability. To address this limitation, we propose PRM-as-a-Judge, a dense evaluation paradigm that leverages Process Reward Models (PRMs) to audit policy execution directly from trajectory videos by estimating task progress from observation sequences. Central to this paradigm is the OPD (Outcome-Process-Diagnosis) metric system, which explicitly formalizes execution quality via a task-aligned progress potential. We characterize dense robotic evaluation through two axiomatic properties: macro-consistency, which requires additive and path-consistent aggregation, and micro-resolution, which requires sensitivity to fine-grained physical evolution. Under this formulation, potential-based PRM judges provide a natural instantiation of dense evaluation, with macro-consistency following directly from the induced scalar potential. We empirically validate the micro-resolution property using RoboPulse, a diagnostic benchmark specifically designed for probing micro-scale progress discrimination, where several trajectory-trained PRM judges outperform discriminative similarity-based methods and general-purpose foundation-model judges. Finally, leveraging PRM-as-a-Judge and the OPD metric system, we conduct a structured audit of mainstream policy paradigms across long-horizon tasks, revealing behavioral signatures and failure modes that are invisible to outcome-only metrics.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTaking Initiative in Human-Robot Action Teams: How Proactive Robot Behaviors Afect Teamwork - Penn State (https://pure.psu.edu/en/publications/taking-initiative-in-human-robot-action-teams-how-proactive-robot/)\nciteturn26search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; We designed and studied proactive robot behaviors in non-dyadic (three humans and a robot) action teams to better understand factors that support the acceptance and effectiveness of proactive robots.Our work will support better human-robot teaming in action teams by addressing gaps in how robot initiative impacts team dynamics, preferences for proactive robot behaviors, and how proactive robots can take initiative based on task states to empower their teammates.\n\n# Taking Initiative in Human-Robot Action Teams: How Proactive Robot Behaviors Afect Teamwork\n\n  * Rabeya Jamshad\n  * , Arthi Haripriyan\n  * , Advika Sonti\n  * , Susan Simkins\n  * , Laurel D. Riek\n\n  * Psychology\n  * Penn State Clinical and Translational Science Institute (CTSI)\n\nResearch output: Chapter in Book/Report/Conference proceeding › Conference contribution\n\n9   Link opens in a new tab Scopus citations\n\n## Abstract\n\nPeople coordinate in action teams to accomplish shared goals in safety- and time-critical contexts such as healthcare and firefighting. Operational failures in these teams can cost lives. Therefore, in our work, we explore how robots that take initiative to support team and task success can augment action teams. We designed and studied proactive robot behaviors in non-dyadic (three humans and a robot) action teams to better understand factors that support the acceptance and effectiveness of proactive robots. Our work will support better human-robot teaming in action teams by addressing gaps in how robot initiative impacts team dynamics, preferences for proactive robot behaviors, and how proactive robots can take initiative based on task states to empower their teammates.\n\nOriginal language  | English (US)\nTitle of host publication  | HRI 2024 Companion - Companion of the 2024 ACM/IEEE International Conference on Human-Robot Interaction\nPublisher  | IEEE Computer Society\nPages  | 559-562\nNumber of pages  | 4\nISBN (Electronic)  | 9798400703232\nDOIs  | \n\n  * https://doi.org/10.1145/3610978.3640640\nState  | Published - Mar 11 2024\n\n### Publication series\n\nName  | ACM/IEEE International Conference on Human-Robot Interaction\nISSN (Electronic)  | 2167-2148\n\n### Conference\n\nConference  | 19th Annual ACM/IEEE International Conference on Human-Robot Interaction, HRI 2024\nCountry/Territory  | United States\nCity  | Boulder\nPeriod  | 3/11/24 → 3/15/24\n\n\n## Access to Document\n\n  * 10.1145/3610978.3640640\n\n## Other files and links\n\n  * Link to publication in Scopus\n\n  * Link to the citations in Scopus\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTaking Initiative in Human-Robot Action Teams: How Proactive Robot Behaviors Affect Teamwork (https://cseweb.ucsd.edu/~lriek/papers/jamshad-HRI24.pdf)\nciteturn26search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.7 years ago; Our work will support better human-robot teaming in action teams by addressing gaps in how robot initiative impacts team dynamics, preferences for proactive robot behaviors, and how proactive robots can take initiative based on task states to empower their teammates. ... Figure 1: Our work aims to support action teams by designing proactive robots that can take initiative to advance team goals.\nTaking Initiative in Human-Robot Action Teams: How Proactive Robot Behaviors Affect Teamwork\n\nRabeya Jamshad  \nUniversity of California San Diego  \nLa Jolla, CA, USA\n\nArthi Haripriyan  \nUniversity of California San Diego  \nLa Jolla, CA, USA\n\nAdvika Sonti  \nUniversity of California San Diego  \nLa Jolla, CA, USA\n\nSusan Simkins  \nPennsylvania State University  \nUniversity Park, PA, USA\n\nLaurel D. Riek  \nUniversity of California San Diego  \nLa Jolla, CA, USA\n\n## ABSTRACT\nPeople coordinate in action teams to accomplish shared goals in safety- and time-critical contexts such as healthcare and firefighting. Operational failures in these teams can cost lives. Therefore, in our work, we explore how robots that take initiative to support team and task success can augment action teams. We designed and studied proactive robot behaviors in non-dyadic (three humans and a robot) action teams to better understand factors that support the acceptance and effectiveness of proactive robots. Our work will support better human-robot teaming in action teams by addressing gaps in how robot initiative impacts team dynamics, preferences for proactive robot behaviors, and how proactive robots can take initiative based on task states to empower their teammates.\n\n## CCS CONCEPTS\n• Human-centered computing → Interaction design; • Computer systems organization → Robotics.\n\n## KEYWORDS\nHuman-Robot Teaming, Groups and Teams, Proactive Robots, Group Dynamics\n\n**ACM Reference Format:**  \nRabeya Jamshad, Arthi Haripriyan, Advika Sonti, Susan Simkins, and Laurel D. Riek. 2024. Taking Initiative in Human-Robot Action Teams: How Proactive Robot Behaviors Affect Teamwork. In *Companion of the 2024 ACM/IEEE International Conference on Human-Robot Interaction (HRI ’24 Companion), March 11–14, 2024, Boulder, CO, USA*. ACM, New York, NY, USA, 4 pages. https://doi.org/10.1145/3610978.3640640\n\n# 1 INTRODUCTION\nCollaborating in teams is an essential aspect of our species. We farm for food, play sports for entertainment and competition, and build bridges and cities to improve the quality of our lives. All these require us to coordinate with one another in a fluent, cohesive, and proactive manner.\n\nResearch reported in this paper is supported by the Office of Naval Research (ONR) under Grant No. N00014-22-1-2813.\n\nThis work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution International 4.0 License.\n\nHRI ’24 Companion, March 11–14, 2024, Boulder, CO, USA  \n© 2024 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).  \nACM ISBN 979-8-4007-0323-2/24/03.  \nhttps://doi.org/10.1145/3610978.3640640\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nFigure 1 shows four photos arranged in a 2×2 collage of a mobile manipulator robot (Stretch) interacting with people in an indoor lab/room setting. Top-left: participants seated around a table during an icebreaker, with the robot nearby. Top-right: participants solving a puzzle while the robot hands over an item. Bottom-left: a person lying on a table while others attend to them, with the robot nearby. Bottom-right: the Stretch robot beside a table, used in a first-aid/safety context.\n</visual_element>\n\nFigure 1: Our work aims to support action teams by designing proactive robots that can take initiative to advance team goals. We explore this within the context of escape rooms using Stretch, a mobile manipulator robot. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRobots That Take Initiative: A Framework for Building and Evaluating Proactive Robots | Cool Papers - Immersive Paper Discovery (https://papers.cool/arxiv/2609.28910)\nciteturn26search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; ## #1 Robots That Take Initiative: A Framework for Building and Evaluating Proactive Robots [PDF^{}] [Copy] [Kimi^{}] [REL] ... We then show that offline evaluation overstates performance in this setting, and contribute a closed-loop evaluation with a human model that adapts to the robot.\n# 2609.28910\n\nTotal: 1\n\n## #1 Robots That Take Initiative: A Framework for Building and Evaluating Proactive Robots [PDF^{}] [Copy] [Kimi^{}] [REL]\n\nAuthors: Maithili Patel, Sonia Chernova\n\nEffective robot assistance beyond narrow roles and repetitive tasks requires robots to be proactive - to decide what needs to be done rather than waiting to be told. While proactivity is increasingly explored, it lacks a unified formulation, and work in the domain is typically evaluated offline against static human models that cannot capture the effect of a robot's actions on the environment and the user's own behavior. We introduce a unified formalism for proactive robot assistance, organize it into three levels, and provide a framework to address the highest level of unprompted proactive assistance. We then show that offline evaluation overstates performance in this setting, and contribute a closed-loop evaluation with a human model that adapts to the robot. Finally, we present a method, GAP, that instantiates our framework, learning from passive observation to anticipate user goals and act. Under closed-loop evaluation, prior state-of-the-art methods collapse, in some cases adding more work than they save, while GAP remains robust and substantially outperforms them.\n\nSubjects: Robotics , Artificial Intelligence\n\nPublish: 2026-09-24 01:42:09 UTC--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPRM-as-a-Judge 1.5: A Toolkit for Robot Process Assessment (https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.14284)\nciteturn26academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Title: PRM-as-a-Judge 1.5: A Toolkit for Robot Process Assessment ... Fine-grained robotic evaluation matters for understanding embodied models, going beyond binary success rates and rule-based process scores.\nTitle: PRM-as-a-Judge 1.5: A Toolkit for Robot Process Assessment\nAuthors: Yuyang Liu, Yanqing Shen, Ruike Chen, Jifan Zhao, Yuxuan Tian, Yichi Zhang, Tianfeng Long, Zixuan Yin, Yipu Wang, Ziheng Qin, Wenxing Tan, Yang Shi, Mingyu Cao, Runze Xiao, Ziqi Wang, Zhixin Yin, Shiwei Chu, Yi-Fan Zhang, Yao Mu, Yuheng Ji, Yihao Wang, Jun Yan, Zhongyuan Wang, Pengwei Wang, Xiaolong Zheng\nDate: Fri Aug 14 13:10:02 2026\n\nFine-grained robotic evaluation matters for understanding embodied models, going beyond binary success rates and rule-based process scores. We present PRM-as-a-Judge 1.5, a toolkit for robot process assessment that turns rollout videos into dense progress curves and derives multiple fine metrics. PRM-as-a-Judge 1.5 introduces three metrics, building on version 1.0, that characterize failure-side progress, post-drawdown recovery, and success-side execution quality, helping users understand embodied model capability. Based on the rollout videos from benchmarks, we perform a comprehensive assessment of the embodied models, providing some fine-grained metric results and key findings. We further introduce RoboPulse++ to evaluate the reliability of process reward models (PRM), providing evaluators with a more accurate testing platform. Moreover, we release a user-friendly assessment suite, including the benchmark, metric implementation, and visualization tools, to support reproducible manipulation process evaluation. We call on the community to rethink how robots are evaluated and establish transparent, procedural, and reproducible assessment as a foundation for the next generation of embodied intelligence.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPRM-as-a-Judge Advanced Guide (https://prm-as-a-judge.github.io/advanced.html)\nciteturn26search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; ### Main View with a Single-Arm Robot Wrist View ... 当复用已有 RoboMeter 服务器时，PRM-as-a-Judge 会检查 `/model_info`，并要求服务器模型路径与 `PRM_PATH` 匹配。\n\nIf this is your first run, complete the Quick Start before changing the defaults described here.\n\n高级指南\n\n# PRM-as-a-Judge 进阶配置\n\n详细介绍如何根据数据形式、计算资源、评测目标和过程奖励模型（Process Reward Model，PRM）的选择调整 PRM-as-a-Judge。\n\n如果尚未完成首次评测，请先按照快速开始运行默认流程，再调整本页介绍的配置。\n\n## Input: Manifest Format\n\nThe default input is a JSONL manifest. Each line describes one rollout case. Paths may be absolute or relative to the manifest file.\n\n### Single View\n    \n    `{\"case_id\":\"case_001\",\"task_name\":\"put_can\",\"task\":\"put the can into the basket\",\"video\":\"videos/case_001.mp4\"}`\n\n### Main View with a Single-Arm Robot Wrist View\n    \n    `{\"case_id\":\"case_002\",\"task_name\":\"put_can\",\"task\":\"put the can into the basket\",\"video\":\"high.mp4\",\"wrist_video\":\"wrist.mp4\"}`\n\n### Main View with Bimanual Robot Wrist Views\n    \n    `{\"case_id\":\"case_003\",\"task_name\":\"put_can\",\"task\":\"put the can into the basket\",\"video\":\"high.mp4\",\"left_wrist_video\":\"left.mp4\",\"right_wrist_video\":\"right.mp4\"}`\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProactive Robots in Human–Robot Action Teams | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/410710849_Proactive_Robots_in_Human-Robot_Action_Teams)\nciteturn26search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; The promise of robots assisting humans in everyday tasks has led to a variety of research questions and challenges in human-robot collaboration.Here, we address the question of whether and when a robot should take initiative during joint human-robot task execution. ... Human-initiated help gives control of the robot action timing to the user; robot-initiated reactive help triggers robot assistance when it detects that the human needs help; robot-initiated proactive help makes the robot help whenever it can.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRobotic assistance in the coordination of patient care - Matthew Gombolay, Xi Jessie Yang, Bradley Hayes, Nicole Seo, Z